  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nSurface high pressure overhead this morning, that was\nresponsible for the widespread frost/freeze, will shift eastward\nthroughout today bringing southerly flow back across the area.\nTemperatures will quickly rebound in response into the 60s. This\nwarming trend will continue through the weekend and into next\nweek given persistent southwesterly flow. Expect highs in the\n70s by Monday and lower 80s by midweek next week.\n\nAttention toward the next precipitation chances remains late\nnext week as a trough digs and deepens across the Western U.S.\nThis system will slowly progress eastward. As the previous\nforecaster noted, this is climatologically a very dry period for\nthe high plains, and it takes a very specific setup to get \ndecent moisture this far west. So history would be generally \nagainst it. However, the slow progression of the system is\nfavorable, but there still remains uncertainty. The biggest \nfactor is the level of return moisture back into the area after \none of the first significant Fall cold frontal passages \nrecently swept across much of the country. Per surface \nobservations, the cold front has tracked well into the Gulf, and\nis expected to cut farther south over the next day or two. So \nit would take a lot for that richer moisture to come back in\ntime for the upcoming system, but it does have several days. \nNevertheless, POPs remain largely unchanged at around 30-40% \ntoward the end of the forecast period. Globals do try and bring \nmeager instability, so thunder could be possible, but widespread\nsoaking rains is still looking unlikely.",
